{
  "id": "calc2.series.alternating-harmonic",
  "course": "calc2",
  "topic": "series",
  "kind": "series_convergence",
  "title": "The alternating harmonic series",
  "prompt": "Does $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}$ converge absolutely, converge conditionally, or diverge?",
  "answer_format": "'converges' or 'diverges' (add 'absolutely' or 'conditionally' when the terms change sign).",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "tags": [
    "alternating series",
    "conditional convergence"
  ],
  "data": {
    "term": "(-1)^(n+1)/n"
  },
  "answer": "converges conditionally",
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "Check the alternating series test, then separately check Σ|aₙ|."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "$\\frac1n$ decreases to $0$, so the series converges by the alternating series test.",
    "But $\\sum \\left|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\right| = \\sum \\frac1n$ is the harmonic series, which diverges.",
    "So the series converges conditionally."
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "openstax-calc2",
    "section": "5.5 Alternating Series",
    "note": "Original problem."
  },
